<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kataghan Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1815 CE – 1859 CE (approx.) · capital Kunduz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Uzbek khanate of northern Afghanistan centred on Kunduz, a slave-raiding power under Murad Beg until absorbed by the Afghan Emirate.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Qataghan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> had settled in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunduz">Kunduz</a> in the 17th and 18th centuries. In the early 19th century their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-beg-of-kunduz">Murad Beg</a> built a powerful state from Kunduz, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-badakhshan">Badakhshan</a> and neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajik</a> districts around 1815 to 1820.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-beg-of-kunduz">Murad Beg</a> ruled with an army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> horsemen and grew rich from raiding and slaving, deporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajiks</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-badakhshan">Badakhshan</a> to the marshy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunduz">Kunduz</a> plain. The British traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-burnes">Alexander Burnes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-moorcroft">William Moorcroft</a> described his court in the 1820s and 1830s. Successors were weaker and paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-beg-of-kunduz">Murad Beg</a>'s death in the 1840s the khanate declined. In 1859 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan's</a> son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-afzal-khan">Muhammad Afzal Khan</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunduz">Kunduz</a> for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-afghanistan">Emirate of Afghanistan</a>, ending its independence.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="khanate, 1815 CE – 1859 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 74 V58 Q50 30 82 58 V74 Z"/><path d="M42 74 V60 H58 V74"/><path d="M30 50 L50 36 L70 50"/><path d="M50 36 V26"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">khanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central Asia and the Steppe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:95.82%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1815 CE – 1859 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kunduz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Murad Beg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Uzbek, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khanate of Kunduz, Kunduz Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1837&amp;focus=kataghan-khanate" aria-label="Kataghan Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6409 854 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kataghan Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6729 1110L6757 1183"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6757 1183h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6729 1110h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6729 1110h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kunduz. <a href="/atlas?year=1837&amp;focus=kataghan-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

